<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meaux</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iatinum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meaux is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It began as Iatinum, the chief town of the Gallic Meldi, and became a bishopric in late antiquity. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of the counts of Meaux, later joined to the county of Champagne, and its most famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> was the seventeenth-century preacher Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meaux.jpg" alt="Meaux" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B25es · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0864ayuntamiento.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
